<header><p class="enc-kicker">Place · region</p><h1 class="enc-title">Basel-Landschaft</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates"><a href="/years/1833">1833</a></span> · <a href="/centuries/19th-century">19th century</a> · Also known as Basel-Country</p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Basel-Landschaft is a canton of north-western <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/switzerland">Switzerland</a>, with its capital at Liestal. It was formed in 1833 when the rural districts, after armed conflict with the city of Basel over political rights, broke away from the canton of Basel. Its voters have repeatedly rejected proposals to reunite with <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/basel-stadt">Basel-Stadt</a>, most recently in 2014.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/basel-landschaft.jpg" alt="Basel-Landschaft" width="330" height="221" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Roland Zumbühl · CC BY-SA 3.0 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Langenbruck-Dorf.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Setting</h2><p>Basel-Landschaft, also called Basel-Country, and informally Baselland or Baselbiet, is one of the 26 cantons of the Swiss Confederation. It is composed of five districts, with Liestal as its capital, and is traditionally considered a half-canton, the other half being <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/basel-stadt">Basel-Stadt</a>. It lies essentially south of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/rhine">Rhine</a> and north of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/jura-mountains">Jura Mountains</a>, in the northwestern corner of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/switzerland">Switzerland</a>, and borders Basel-Stadt, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/aargau">Aargau</a>, Solothurn and Jura, with international borders with <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/france">France</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/germany">Germany</a>. The Ergolz and the Birs drain its lands. Its shape is very irregular, and its borders cut across several towns.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">History</h2><p>In Roman times the area was a centre of Roman activity. The remains of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/augusta-raurica">Augusta Raurica</a>, in what is now Augst, show a city of about 20,000 people around 200 CE, and the site includes the best-preserved amphitheatre north of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/alps">Alps</a>. With <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/basel-stadt">Basel-Stadt</a>, Basel-Landschaft formed the historic canton of Basel, which joined the Old Swiss Confederacy in 1501. Until the end of the 16th century most of its land belonged to the city of Basel, on which the countryside was economically dependent, and the castles and residences of Basel merchants dominated much of the landscape. After <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/napoleon">Napoleon</a>'s visit in 1798 the country achieved equality with the city.</p><p>After 1830 political quarrels and armed conflict broke out in the canton, partly over the rights of the rural population. They culminated in the uprising of 1833, including the Battle of the Hülftenschanz near Frenkendorf, and led to the separation of Basel-Landschaft from the city on 26 August 1833.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">The place today</h2><p>A movement for reunification gained momentum after 1900, when many parts of Basel-Landschaft became industrialised. The two half cantons agreed in principle to merge, but in 1969 the people of Basel-Landschaft voted down the proposal, perhaps because the economic gap between the two had closed. Since then they have signed agreements to co-operate, such as the canton's contribution to the University of Basel since 1976. In 1988 the canton wrote into its constitution the aim of recognition of the two Basels as full cantons. The population is predominantly German-speaking.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Basel-Landschaft” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>Restored in 1803, the canton split in 1833 after civil conflict into Basel-Stadt and <b>Basel-Landschaft</b>, which remain separate half-cantons.</blockquote><a href="/state/canton-of-basel">Canton of Basel</a></li></ul></section>
